WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:02.414
This is a hardness tester.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.283
Hardness is a material's resistance to being dented.

3
00:00:07.031 --> 00:00:10.735
The tester presses a hard tip into the surface with a set force,

4
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:12.140
and measures the dent.

5
00:00:12.867 --> 00:00:15.189
The smaller the dent, the harder the material.

6
00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:19.461
Brinell presses in a ball and measures the width of the dent:

7
00:00:19.601 --> 00:00:21.749
for softer steels and castings.

8
00:00:22.465 --> 00:00:27.620
Vickers presses in a diamond pyramid and measures the diagonals: for any material,

9
00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:28.862
thin layers too.

10
00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:34.202
Rockwell C measures how deep a diamond cone sinks: the scale for hardened steel.

11
00:00:35.204 --> 00:00:38.687
Mild steel is about 120 HB.

12
00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:42.777
A hardened tool steel reaches 60 HRC or more.

13
00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:47.894
Converting between the scales goes by tables, and stays an estimate.

14
00:00:48.946 --> 00:00:52.359
Harder steel is usually stronger and more wear resistant.

15
00:00:53.132 --> 00:00:57.079
The test is quick and cheap, so it is a standard incoming check.

16
00:00:57.767 --> 00:00:59.346
But how do you make steel hard?

17
00:01:00.152 --> 00:01:00.721
With heat.

18
00:01:02.044 --> 00:01:06.792
Steel with enough carbon, roughly 0.3 percent or more, can be hardened.

19
00:01:07.524 --> 00:01:11.668
The hoist lowers this shaft into the furnace, and the lid goes shut.

20
00:01:12.768 --> 00:01:18.225
The shaft heats up to roughly 800 to 900 degrees, depending on the steel.

21
00:01:18.972 --> 00:01:23.012
Then the carbon dissolves in the iron: the steel turns to austenite.

22
00:01:24.145 --> 00:01:26.351
Then quenching: cooling very fast.

23
00:01:27.103 --> 00:01:30.621
Within seconds the shaft goes from the furnace into the oil;

24
00:01:30.737 --> 00:01:33.187
water or a polymer solution works too.

25
00:01:33.868 --> 00:01:38.489
The carbon has nowhere to go, and is trapped in a strained structure: martensite.

26
00:01:39.497 --> 00:01:43.863
The steel is now very hard, but also brittle: it breaks without warning.

27
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:48.574
The same happens in the HAZ of a weld that cools too fast;

28
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.035
that is why thick steel is preheated.

29
00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.077
Quenching comes at a price.

30
00:01:54.841 --> 00:01:59.183
The outside cools before the core, and that builds up large stresses.

31
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.218
A long shaft can bend: distortion.

32
00:02:03.251 --> 00:02:06.143
Where the stress piles up, the steel can crack.

33
00:02:06.907 --> 00:02:11.760
A quench crack usually starts at a sharp corner or a sudden change in thickness,

34
00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:13.223
like here at the collar.

35
00:02:14.211 --> 00:02:16.753
The faster it cools, the bigger the risk.

36
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.950
Water is the harshest, oil the mildest,

37
00:02:20.124 --> 00:02:23.003
and a polymer solution usually sits in between.

38
00:02:24.034 --> 00:02:27.459
What helps: a generous radius instead of a sharp corner,

39
00:02:27.645 --> 00:02:29.247
and you settle that on the drawing.

40
00:02:30.003 --> 00:02:31.907
Long shafts are quenched hanging.

41
00:02:33.266 --> 00:02:37.852
So tempering follows straight away: the shaft goes into a furnace again,

42
00:02:38.108 --> 00:02:42.787
now typically between 150 and 650 degrees.

43
00:02:43.792 --> 00:02:45.696
The martensite relaxes a little.

44
00:02:46.411 --> 00:02:50.718
You give up some hardness, and gain toughness: taking a blow without cracking.

45
00:02:51.729 --> 00:02:55.491
Low tempering keeps the steel hard, for tools such as a chisel.

46
00:02:56.315 --> 00:03:00.390
High tempering makes it tough, for shafts and bolts that take shocks.

47
00:03:01.385 --> 00:03:05.925
Quench and temper together set the properties of shafts, gears and tools.

48
00:03:06.646 --> 00:03:11.963
A quenched part that is never tempered can crack by itself, sometimes days later.

49
00:03:13.281 --> 00:03:16.752
Annealing does the opposite: heating and cooling very slowly,

50
00:03:16.915 --> 00:03:18.680
often in the furnace itself.

51
00:03:19.367 --> 00:03:22.792
The steel becomes soft, and easy to machine or form.

52
00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:30.589
Stress relieving is gentler: for steel typically around 550 to 650 degrees,

53
00:03:30.751 --> 00:03:33.340
below the temperature where the structure changes.

54
00:03:34.057 --> 00:03:36.135
It removes internal stresses.

55
00:03:37.299 --> 00:03:38.518
Take a welded part.

56
00:03:39.278 --> 00:03:41.472
Welding locks stresses into the steel.

57
00:03:42.189 --> 00:03:46.635
Machine a face, and that stress is released, and the whole part warps.

58
00:03:47.661 --> 00:03:51.248
Stress relieve first, then machine: then it stays straight.

59
00:03:52.019 --> 00:03:53.517
Put that step in the routing.

60
00:03:54.796 --> 00:03:57.478
Sometimes you want a hard surface against wear,

61
00:03:57.676 --> 00:03:59.603
on a tough core that takes the blows.

62
00:04:00.329 --> 00:04:01.478
That is case hardening.

63
00:04:02.214 --> 00:04:04.757
Gears and bearing seats are treated this way.

64
00:04:05.776 --> 00:04:11.314
In carburising, carbon soaks a little way into the surface at about 900 degrees.

65
00:04:12.038 --> 00:04:15.277
After the quench, only that skin becomes really hard.

66
00:04:16.378 --> 00:04:21.278
Nitriding brings nitrogen into the surface, at about 500 degrees.

67
00:04:22.075 --> 00:04:25.129
There is no quench, so the part distorts very little.

68
00:04:26.175 --> 00:04:30.645
In induction hardening a coil heats only the outer layer in a few seconds,

69
00:04:30.819 --> 00:04:32.781
and the quench follows straight after.

70
00:04:33.517 --> 00:04:36.176
The core stays cool, and so stays tough.

71
00:04:37.483 --> 00:04:43.903
The drawing then says, for example: carburised and hardened, 58 to 62 HRC,

72
00:04:44.042 --> 00:04:47.351
case depth 0.8 to 1.2 millimetres.

73
00:04:48.101 --> 00:04:50.237
A spec to check, not decoration.

74
00:04:51.239 --> 00:04:54.536
Gears from a new supplier wear out within a few weeks.

75
00:04:55.242 --> 00:05:00.327
The incoming check measures 48 HRC at the surface: far too soft.

76
00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:05.009
Was the carburising too short, did the surface lose carbon,

77
00:05:05.183 --> 00:05:06.611
or was the quench too slow?

78
00:05:07.317 --> 00:05:09.732
Quarantine the stock, cut one gear through,

79
00:05:09.952 --> 00:05:12.355
and measure with Vickers from the surface inward.

80
00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.190
Then you see the real case depth.

81
00:05:16.218 --> 00:05:18.818
And ask the supplier for the furnace records and

82
00:05:18.865 --> 00:05:21.187
the heat-treatment certificate of that batch.
